<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>BaseApi.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>BaseApi.Tests</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <UseMicrosoftTestingPlatformRunner>true</UseMicrosoftTestingPlatformRunner>
    <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>true</TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THREE referenced projects each contribute an appsettings.json to this output directory
    — BaseApi.Service, Orchestrator and Processor.Sample — and they all land on the same
    path. Which one wins is decided by file timestamps, so the ambient configuration these
    tests build hosts against was silently non-deterministic, and `dotnet publish` of this
    project would fail outright with NETSDK1152 on the duplicate.

    No test depends on it: every one that builds a host supplies its own keys through
    AddInMemoryCollection, which is applied after the file and therefore wins. So the fix is
    to take none of them rather than to pick one — an ambient file nothing reads is just a
    trap for the next person who adds a test and wonders where a value came from.
  -->
  <!--
    KNOWN, UNFIXED: three referenced projects each contribute an appsettings.json to this
    output directory — BaseApi.Service, Orchestrator and Processor.Sample — and all three
    land on the same path. Which one wins is decided by file timestamps.

    Harmless today, and the reason is worth stating so nobody "fixes" it by picking one:
    every test that builds a host supplies its own keys via AddInMemoryCollection, which is
    applied after the file and therefore wins. What it does break is `dotnet publish` of this
    project, which fails with NETSDK1152 on the duplicate. Nothing publishes a test project,
    so that has never been hit.

    Two removals were tried and neither works: an ItemGroup Remove on
    AllItemsFullPathWithTargetPath AfterTargets="GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems" (too late —
    the item is already resolved), and one on _SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory /
    ReferenceCopyLocalPaths BeforeTargets="_CopyOutOfDateSourceItemsToOutputDirectory" (wrong
    list — transitive content arrives by another path). The next thing to try is a
    post-Build Delete task, which is crude but deterministic. Recorded here so the next person
    starts from attempt three.
  -->


  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="xunit.runner.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Include="Resilience\Fixtures\*.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3.assert" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="NSubstitute" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BaseApi.Core\BaseApi.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BaseApi.Service\BaseApi.Service.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BaseConsole.Core\BaseConsole.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Messaging.Transport\Messaging.Transport.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BaseProcessor.Core\BaseProcessor.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Processor.Sample\Processor.Sample.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Orchestrator\Orchestrator.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
